# Import of the Lush implementation (929 MB on disk, ~99.8% of it saved model
# checkpoints under Training/). Only the `dataseq` ancestry and enough of its
# surroundings to read it are wanted, so the rule is deny-by-default: nothing
# here is tracked unless it is named below.
#
# This file lives in `.scratch/hmm-lush/` rather than the repo root so that
# deleting `.scratch/` at the end of the merge removes the rules along with what
# they describe -- see `.scratch/README.md`.
#
# The repo-root `.gitignore` still applies underneath these rules, and is what
# keeps the 42 `.DS_Store` files out even inside the re-included directories. Do
# not restate those here; a negation added below could re-admit them by accident.
#
# Unlike the MelodyHPO import, this tree carries no `.git` and no nested
# `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`, so neither of the two renames recorded in
# `.scratch/README.md` was needed. It does carry CVS control directories under
# `Code/_Old Lisp Code/`, which git does not treat specially and which the
# catch-all excludes anyway.

# --- deny everything at this level -------------------------------------------
/*
!/.gitignore
!/.gitkeep
!/Code/
!/Training/

# Our own writing about the import -- an ANALYSIS.md and anything joining it.
# This negation is easy to forget and fails silently: a file written here without
# it is simply invisible to `git status`, so the work looks committed and is not.
!/*.md

# Our Python transliteration of the Lush sources (goal 3, subgoal 3). Source only:
# `/translation/*` re-excludes the directory's contents so `__pycache__/` and the
# `.pyc` files inside it stay out -- a directory that is excluded cannot have its
# children re-included, which is what makes the single `*.py` negation sufficient.
# The same silent-failure warning as above applies, and it very nearly fired here:
# these files were written, verified to run, and were invisible to `git status`
# until `git check-ignore` was run on them deliberately.
!/translation/
/translation/*
!/translation/*.py

# --- Code: source only, and only the live source -----------------------------
# `*.lsh` and nothing else. This is what keeps out, without naming them:
#   C/**/*.o          compiled objects for two dead architectures,
#                     powerpc-apple-darwin8 and x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
#   C/*.c             machine output. Every one opens "WARNING: Automatically
#                     generated code ... by the DH compiler" -- these are Lush's
#                     compiler emitting C from the .lsh beside them, not a
#                     hand-written C layer, so they are a build artefact and
#                     teach nothing the .lsh does not say better
#   *.lsh~,           editor backups and a stray "util.lsh copy"
#   "*.lsh copy"
/Code/*
!/Code/*.lsh
!/Code/hmm-test-view

# The loader `load-hmm.lsh` names these three directories and no others; they are
# the whole live library.
!/Code/SeqData/
!/Code/HMMlib/
!/Code/Utility/

# The merge target proper: dsource-seq.lsh (the container), seq-state.lsh (a
# single sequence), format-sds.lsh (the vocabulary builder and serializer).
/Code/SeqData/*
!/Code/SeqData/*.lsh

# The consumer. `dataseq`'s shape here is only legible against what reads it, and
# this is additionally the translation target of the *next* package in PRD order.
/Code/HMMlib/*
!/Code/HMMlib/*.lsh

# Helpers the above libload -- `mkdir-on-need`, `blank-str`, `setqC`, `member-pos`.
# Without them the container's code does not resolve. The `*.lsh` glob also admits
# three `LU-test-*.lsh` files that `load-hmm.lsh` does not load; they are kept
# because they are the only tests that exist anywhere in this tree, and naming
# `util.lsh` and `mc.lsh` individually to exclude them would trade that for nothing.
/Code/Utility/*
!/Code/Utility/*.lsh

# --- Training: the driver scripts and two dataset specimens ------------------
# `ds-format` runs format-sds over a `.sds` directory; the `hmm-train-*` pair are
# the entry points. Keep all four: the `-nw` ("no window") variants are not
# duplicates -- they drop the GUI and run the topology search as a bare loop
# (`suggest-move` / `keep-model`), which is the clearest statement of it anywhere
# in the tree.
/Training/*
!/Training/ds-format
!/Training/hmm-train-load
!/Training/hmm-train-load-nw
!/Training/hmm-train-new
!/Training/hmm-train-new-nw

# Two `.sds` directories, tracked whole. A `.sds` is not a file but a directory
# -- `_alphabet`, `_alphabet_size`, `_size`, `_seq_sizes`, `_seq_size_max`,
# `_raw_data`, and one `<i>.seq` per sequence -- and it is the only specification
# of the on-disk format that exists, since no document describes it. Tracking one
# *partially* would be worse than not tracking it: `_size` is a count the loader
# trusts, so a directory missing some of its `.seq` files is not a smaller
# example but a corrupt one.
#
#   set11a_dInt.sds   7 files, 16 KB. One sequence, 1449 symbols, and a real
#                     25-symbol alphabet of multi-character strings (`+4th`,
#                     `-8ve`, and bar-quoted `|E. -2nd|`, `|H _|`) -- which is
#                     the case `dataseq` exists to handle.
#   set01z0_100.sds   107 files, 11 KB. A synthetic {a,b} toy, and the only
#                     specimen showing many *ragged* sequences: 100 of them,
#                     lengths 2 to 20, against `_seq_size_max` 20. That raggedness
#                     is what the loader packs into a dense `size x seq_size_max`
#                     int matrix, so this is the evidence for how the original
#                     padded -- the ADR 0011 question this branch has to answer.
!/Training/set01z0/
/Training/set01z0/*
!/Training/set01z0/set01z0_100.sds/
!/Training/set11a_dInt/
/Training/set11a_dInt/*
!/Training/set11a_dInt/set11a_dInt.sds/

# --- deliberately not tracked, with reasons ----------------------------------
# Named here rather than left to the catch-all so that the reasoning survives.
#
#   Training/<all others>   ~928 MB of saved checkpoints from 2008-2011 training
#                     runs: 8180 `.dot` graphs, 4179 each of transition_p /
#                     state_p / output_p / _training_log / _total_dl, 4090 `.xls`.
#                     Outputs of the algorithm being translated, not inputs to it,
#                     and unreadable without the model code that wrote them.
#   Code/_Obsolete/   An earlier generation of the same HMM library (hmm-bw,
#                     hmm-ss, hmm-sm). Superseded by HMMlib/ and not loaded by
#                     load-hmm.lsh.
#   "Code/_Old Lisp Code/"
#                     A Common Lisp predecessor, interpreted and never compiled.
#                     Reference only, and the owner's judgement is that it holds
#                     nothing the Lush version does not. Carries CVS/ metadata
#                     from its original repository.
